      "text": "To convert the point \\((0, -3\\sqrt{3}, 3)\\) from rectangular coordinates to spherical coordinates \\((\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)\\), we need to use the following formulas:\n\n1. \\(\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}\\)\n2. \\(\\theta = \\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\) (with consideration of the quadrant)\n3. \\(\\phi = \\arccos\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho}\\right)\\)\n\nGiven the point \\((0, -3\\sqrt{3}, 3)\\):\n\n1. Calculate \\(\\rho\\):\n\\[\n\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2} = \\sqrt{0^2 + (-3\\sqrt{3})^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{0 + 27 + 9} = \\sqrt{36} = 6\n\\]\n\n2. Calculate \\(\\theta\\):\nSince the \\(x\\)-coordinate is 0 and the \\(y\\)-coordinate is \\(-3\\sqrt{3}\\), the point lies on the negative \\(y\\)-axis. Therefore, \\(\\theta\\) is:\n\\[\n\\theta = \\pi + \\arctan\\left(\\frac{|-3\\sqrt{3}|}{0}\\right) = \\pi + \\frac{\\pi}{2} = \\frac{3\\pi}{2}\n\\]\n\n3. Calculate \\(\\phi\\):\n\\[\n\\phi = \\arccos\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho}\\right) = \\arccos\\left(\\frac{3}{6}\\right) = \\arccos\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the spherical coordinates are:\n\\[\n(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) = \\left(6, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left(6, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)}\n\\]",
